  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a compact upper level shortwave trough lifting \nnortheast through the high plains of southeast Colorado/southwest \nKansas, responsible for a few ongoing isolated areas of sprinkles/light\nrain. Near the surface, a lee side trough of low pressure remains\nanchored in eastern Colorado.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are likely early in the period despite the \nSREF indicating another series of H5 vort maxima associated with a\nweak perturbation lifting northeast out of the Desert Southwest into\nwestern Kansas late Monday night/early Tuesday morning. Minimal moisture\nreturn will elevate surface dewpoints somewhat, but do little to\nbring about much in the way of instability. Still, a few pockets\nof sprinkles/light rain cannot be ruled out toward daybreak Tuesday\nas the aforementioned shortwave trough interacts with a weak lee\nside trough axis in vicinity of the Colorado line. Otherwise, expect\na drier pattern to persist through mid-week as ridging aloft begins\nto develop and amplify across the Western High Plains.\n\nSeasonal temperatures are forecast today as prevailing southerlies \nhelp reinforce a milder air mass across the area, supporting H85 \ntemperatures holding around 10C in central Kansas to near 15C in \nextreme southwest Kansas. With the latest HREF painting a widespread \n80-90% probability of temperatures exceeding 65F to a 70-90% probability\nof temperatures nudging above 70F near and along the Oklahoma line\nin south central Kansas and much of southwest Kansas, look for afternoon\nhighs generally up into the upper 60s(F) to the lower 70s(F). More\nwidespread 70s(F) are expected Tuesday as ridging aloft begins to\ndevelop and amplify across the Western High Plains.",
